  "query_preview": "(Today through Thursday)...\nIssued at 226 AM EST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nEarly This Morning through Today...\n\nAn upper level low was across eastern Kentucky early this morning, \nwith a surface low across eastern Ohio. The surface low had a trough \nextending west into Indiana. Drier air working into the upper system \nhas allowed precipitation to become more banded with areas of dry or \nvery light precipitation in between.\n\nPartially melted snowflakes have been noted locally with some \nautomated sites reporting light snow. Soundings show only the near \nsurface layer of the atmosphere is above freezing. Surface \ntemperatures were in the middle and upper 30s.\n\nPrecipitation will continue across much of the area through about \n12Z then diminish some as forcing weakens with the system beginning \nto exit. Surface temperatures will slowly fall some, but they will \nremain above freezing. This will allow higher odds of snow mixing in \nwith the rain, especially north and east. In any stronger bands, \nprecipitation may become fully snow for brief periods, which would \ncause some slushy very light accumulations. With temperatures \nremaining above freezing, impacts would be low to nonexistent. \n\nTemperatures gradually rising after sunrise will end the threat of \nsnow mixing in, with all rain expected by 15Z or so. Overall \ncoverage of precipitation will diminish into early afternoon as the \nsystem exits. However, guidance is showing a band of rain developing \nin the east this afternoon as forcing rotates around the edge of the \nsystem. Will bring PoPs back up for a while this afternoon east.\n\nHighs today will be in the lower 40s.\n\nTonight through Saturday...\n\nLingering light rain can't be ruled out far east early tonight, so \nwill have some low PoPs there. A jet streak and weak upper trough \nwill bring lift in the mid and upper layers of the atmosphere, \nincreasing the clouds again overnight into Saturday morning. Forcing \nis weak enough and the lower atmosphere dry enough that no rain is \nexpected with this.\n\nSaturday night through Sunday night...\n\nAn upper trough and a deepening to sub-1000mb surface low will \nimpact the area later Saturday night into Sunday night. A \nstrengthening upper jet will accompany the system. Good moisture \ntransport into the area will provide enough moisture to work with \nstrong forcing from the aforementioned features to produce \nwidespread rain once again on Sunday. Will go high PoPs all areas \nduring the day Sunday. \n\nThe PV anomaly associated with the system will work with the strong \nbaroclinic zone to produce frontogenetical forcing. This will create \nsome bands of heavier rainfall on Sunday. At the moment will be \ngoing with around a half inch or rain, but depending on where the \ndeeper moisture ends up and the location of the banded heavier rain, \namounts closer to an inch may occur.\n\nThermal profiles show rain as the precipitation type Saturday night \nthrough Sunday, but some cooler air working in behind the surface \nlow Sunday night may allow some snow to mix in before the rain ends.\n\nMonday through Wednesday...\n\nWeak systems moving through generally zonal flow aloft will remain \nnorth and south of the area. Surface high pressure will be the main \ninfluence, keeping the area dry. Temperatures will moderate into the \n50s by Wednesday.\n\nThursday and beyond...\n\nAn upper trough and surface low pressure system will bring rain \nchances to the area. Current timing for best chances for rain looks \nto be later Thursday into Friday. Southerly winds ahead of the \nsurface system will help bring in a return to temperatures in the \n60s for parts of the area.",
